Ladybird preserves a lone specified sticky inset when the box is
larger than its scrollport. It reduces the effective end inset only
when both sides of that axis specify an inset. This is an explicit
compatibility deviation from CSS Positioned Layout, whose end-inset
reduction rule treats an auto inset as zero without this exception.

Blink applies the same two-sided condition in LayoutBoxModelObject::
ComputeStickyPositionConstraints. Gecko checks both computed offsets
in StickyScrollContainer::ComputeStickyLimits, and WebKit checks both
anchor edges in RenderBoxModelObject::computeStickyPositionConstraints.
Their single-inset cases retain the specified attachment even when
the opposite edge extends outside the sticky view rectangle.

For the 200px scrollport case, Chrome places a 260px bottom:10px box
at top -70px and bottom 190px after scrolling. Both axes behave the
same in horizontal-tb, vertical-rl, and vertical-lr, while controls
with both insets specified keep the start-side attachment.
